It makes the meaning of simulation success governable. Use Case 2 — Future Space Mission Where Complete Reality Does Not Yet Exist
A space organization is preparing a mission to an environment that cannot be completely reproduced on Earth and has never been experienced in the exact intended mission configuration.
Simulation becomes essential.
Spacecraft behavior, navigation, landing, environmental interaction, equipment failure, mission contingencies, crew behavior, communication delays, and emergency conditions may all be simulated before the mission exists as Operational Reality.
SIMULOS® provides the governance structure for establishing:
what is being simulated;
why;
which aspects of the future environment are represented;
which assumptions substitute for unavailable knowledge;
which models and data create the representation;
which scenarios must be explored;
how executions are controlled;
what the resulting outputs mean;
which parts can be correlated with existing reality;
what evidential weight those results may legitimately carry;
and which assumptions or reality changes would require resimulation.
The simulation can therefore provide highly valuable evidence without being represented as proof of conditions that have never yet existed.
Where reality cannot yet be fully observed, simulation may extend knowledge—but governance must define where that knowledge ends. The Long-Term Vision

An autonomous industrial machine identifies a required resource, communicates with another machine or digital service, initiates a transaction, receives the resource or service, generates a Value change, and records the interaction without direct human execution.
Several technical messages may exist.
Several AI or machine actors may participate.
The transaction may cross organizational and national infrastructure.
TREGA™ provides the governance structure for establishing:
what occurrence constitutes the Event;
within what Context it exists;
how the Event is formed and bounded;
when and where it occurs;
which machine, platform, organizational, or human actors materially participate;
which governed Value result belongs to the Event;
how the Event relates to preceding or subsequent machine Events;
whether the Event is sufficiently governed to become tax-relevant;
how it becomes a governed Event Record;
and how its lifecycle remains reconstructable if information later changes.
The resulting Event may then be evaluated independently by applicable jurisdiction-specific taxation systems.
The autonomous machine does not need to become a taxpayer merely because it participated in the Event.
Machine Participation may become part of Event Reality—but legal taxpayer status remains a jurisdictional determination.
The Long-Term Vision
Economic activity is moving from human-readable transactions toward increasingly distributed, automated, autonomous, and machine-generated Events.

