whether an action degraded another value dimension;
whether value reached zero;
whether lost value remains recoverable;
whether restoration represents recovery or creation of new value;
and whether a prior value lifecycle has legitimately ended.
VFM® can provide a governance layer through which AI systems reason about those distinctions without reducing every decision to a single monetary or optimization variable.
Combined with AIG®, ASGA™, GOA™, ORA™, VALIDOS®, AGA™, INTEGROS®, ADIT®, CLIA®, MGIA™, SIMULOS®, and OBIDENITY®, VFM® can contribute to future systems capable of reasoning not merely about what action is possible, but about what value exists, what value may be affected, and what value consequences an action may produce.
Future Economic and Tax System Application
VFM® can also provide a methodological layer for future economic, accounting, fiscal, or taxation architectures.
Traditional taxation frequently begins after an economically relevant event has already been legally classified.
A future value-aware system could instead distinguish:
Value Origin → Value Emergence → Value Recognition → Value State → Value Change → Value Development → Value Degradation → Value Nullification → Value Restoration → Value Extinction
This does not mean VFM® determines taxation.
Tax Governance ≠ Value Lifecycle Governance.

Rather, VFM® can provide a standardized value lifecycle layer upon which specialized tax governance can determine which value events are legally relevant, attributable, measurable, taxable, exempt, deferred, restored, transferred, or extinguished.
Combined with identity, provenance, accountability, operational reality, validation, evidence, and classification architectures, this could support future systems in which taxation responds more precisely to the actual governed lifecycle of economic value.
Enterprise and Industrial Application
VFM® can be applied to:
industrial machinery and productive assets;
critical infrastructure;
data and information assets;
software and digital platforms;
intellectual property;
organizational capabilities;
technology portfolios;
research and development;
AI investments;
supply chains;
energy systems;
public infrastructure;
investment portfolios;
stranded assets;
legacy systems;
and long-lived strategic resources.
Instead of asking only:
“What is this worth today?”
VFM® enables a broader governance sequence:
Where does its value exist?
Where did that value originate?
How did it emerge?
Has it been legitimately recognized?
What state is it currently in?
What changed?
Is it developing or degrading?
Has value reached zero?
Can previously existing value be restored?
Or has that value lifecycle legitimately ended?
Multi-Dimensional Value Principle
The same object may simultaneously carry multiple forms of value.

If genuinely new value later appears after legitimate extinction, that value may enter through Value Emergence as a new lifecycle rather than being falsely represented as restoration of the extinct value.
Architectural Structure
Architecture: VFM® — Value Flow Mechanisms Architecture Category: Governance & Enforcement Subcategory: Value Lifecycle Governance Core Parent Standards: 11 Core Modules: 55 Architecture Model: Lifecycle-Based · Modular · Recursive · Cross-Domain Primary Governance Domain: Value Lifecycle Governance
The architecture deliberately separates governable spaces that can independently change or fail.
A system may:
identify the wrong value context;
confuse object origin with value origin;
fail to distinguish emerging value from existing value;
recognize value without sufficient basis;
misidentify the current value state;
confuse an external event with the resulting value change;
confuse value creation with value development;
treat degradation as nullification;
treat zero value as extinction;
treat new value as restored value;
or declare extinction while legitimate recoverability still exists.
VFM® treats these not as one generic value management problem, but as distinct value lifecycle governance spaces requiring distinct methodological boundaries.
Foundational Architecture Principles
Value exists within context.
Value Context ≠ Value State.
Object Origin ≠ Value Origin.
Value Emergence ≠ Value Recognition.
Value Recognition ≠ Classification.

VNS separates these conditions and establishes a governed basis for determining when existing positive value has actually ceased to remain positive.
Governance Flow
Existing Positive Value → Value Nullification Condition → Value Nullification Threshold → DeMonet™ Mechanism → Value Nullification Determination → Zero-Value Condition → ZVA™ — Zero Value Asset™ where applicable
VFM® Governance Flow Position
Value Context → Value Origin → Value Emergence → Value Recognition → Value State → Value Change → Value Development → Value Degradation → Value Nullification → Value Restoration
VNS therefore governs one of the most important boundary transitions in the VFM® lifecycle:
Positive Value → Zero Value.
What VNS Helps Organizations Govern
VNS provides a structured method for determining:
whether positive value previously existed;
which value dimension is being examined;
which Value Context applies;
what condition may cause nullification;
where the applicable zero-value threshold lies;
whether that threshold has actually been reached;
whether nullification is temporary or permanent;
whether the entire Value Object or only one value dimension has reached zero;
and
whether the resulting condition may legitimately enter a ZVA™ state.
This prevents a severe reduction in value from automatically being classified as complete value loss.
DeMonet™
VNS is the architectural home of DeMonet™.

Its operational space includes: context change detection; contextual change identification; change relevance; change materiality; changed contextual relationships; scope-impacting changes; organizational changes; operational changes; transactional changes; digital and platform changes; technological changes; infrastructure changes; autonomous-system changes; value-related contextual changes; newly discovered context; contextual correction; contextual supersession; historical contextual preservation.
ECCHM governs change to Event Context.
It does not govern the complete lifecycle continuity of that context.
Operational Scope
ECCHM determines whether a detected contextual change materially affects the governed representation required to understand the occurrence.
A contextual change may:
have no material effect;
modify an existing contextual element;
introduce a new contextual element;
remove the relevance of an existing element;
alter a contextual relationship;
require Context Scope reconsideration;
or
supersede a previously governed contextual state.
Not every system update, organizational change, data correction, technical migration, or newly available record requires a material change to Event Context.
ECCHM governs only changes capable of materially affecting contextual understanding.
Context Change Principle
The foundational rule is:
Context must be capable of changing without allowing the past to be silently rewritten.

